INT: What was that like for you as a child?

AH: It was very frightening. We used to go to the makeshift bomb shelters, which were in the cellars under the buildings. Ordinarily, those cellars would contain potatoes and coal for the winter. They were huge. They weren't clean, so we used to go there when the alerts-- the sirens of the impending bombing used to come. We used to run to those shelters, and were always covered with dust of coal, because it was there still from the coal.

On the one hand, this was my opportunity for the first time to meet our neighbors. On the other hand, we were sitting there, and we used to listen to the airplanes going by. And they were always experts, and they knew, those are ours, those are theirs.

INT: How could they tell?

AH: Hm?

INT: How could they tell?

AH: I don't know. You know, they-- obviously they were, if I remember correctly, the heavy Messerschmitts that were the bombers and others that I don't remember. But they were always experts. They could tell by the sound of the plane what it was.

But more frightening was when you could hear the heavy-- the hiss of a heavy bomb coming down. And-- and you were just sitting there and listening. Is it going to fall here? Are we going to be buried alive here? What's happening?

And then-- boom! And the whole building, the cellar, would shake. All there was is very specific hiss of a Molotov cocktail that was going, like-- shh-- hiss, like this. And they were exploding with like a champagne bottle's corks. And they had phosphorus things, and they would light on contact. We didn't see it when we were in the cellar, but when we came out, we could see things burning through the contact. And it was very frightening.

And I remember from time to time in the-- when there was no bombing. Incidentally, the sirens were not necessarily accurate, you know. They would start when the bombing already started, or they would start when the bombing didn't start. It was frightening.

We lived on the third story of a three-story building. And one day, my mother and I went up to the apartment to water the plants and to get washed a little bit. And as we were coming down, on the second floor approximately, there was a big bang, and the whole house shook.

We ran to the shelter, and when we came back, we found out that the shrapnel fell into our apartment and made a big hole in the balcony door. And the whole balcony fell through, right through to the first floor. In other words, if we would have been in the apartment a little bit longer, it probably would hit us.

So this is what it was like. And then one day, the building across our street was bombed, and it was just horrible. Half of the building was-- fell down. And the rest that was standing just showed what used to be other people's apartments. You could see bus stops hanging in the air and wallpaper, shreds of wallpaper, even pictures. Excuse me.

And this was just across the street. The building, was which I lived all my life, was suddenly gone. Another thing I remember on the street-- there was a dead horse that must have been hit by flying shrapnel. It was-- it was terrible-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and very frightening.

INT: How were you able to get food?

AH: From time to time, there were bakeries. And I think that Isabela or my father-- somebody used to go to these bakeries and bring bread. Other than that, we knew that the war was coming. So we had some stored food, like rice and flour. And my mother used to bake bread from the stores that we had. I remember also that we had stores of sardines and chocolate. The rest we didn't have.

INT: Was your father still able to work?

AH: Not during the bombing, no no. No. You know, nobody was able to work. Everything was disrupted.

INT: And how long was this period?

AH: I believe it was about two weeks-- two, three weeks.

INT: And then what happened?

AH: And then, as I told you before, you know, the Germans came. Warsaw was without electricity, without water.

[PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

