AH: Well, as I mentioned before, we had to give away our furs. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I don't remember the sequence exactly, but what happened was the-- the Jews were not allowed to be on the street after certain hours. The Jews were obliged to wear white armbands with a blue Star of David, Magen David, on the left arm. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] That--

INT: What was that like for you? Who gave you the arm band?

AH: It was very interesting because [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the situation was awkward in terms of living. The Jews couldn't-- they were fired from their position in non-Jewish or public offices. So-- all there was is scramble to make a living. And it's interesting that-- that there was the whole industry developed about the armbands. My mother made us the white armbands, and she embroidered the light blue Magen David on it.

But there was a whole industry when people started to manufacture wholesale armbands made out of celluloid, if you know what celluloid was. Celluloid is precursor of plastic. Those were permanent in the sense that you can just wipe it off. You didn't have to wash them and iron them. They were made out of paper in case you forgot yours, so you could buy it on the street made out of paper. It was a whole industry about the armband.

To me, at that time I was then-- what? 12, 13. It was a badge of honor. I didn't recognize the intention of demeaning-- demeaning Jews as such. I didn't mind having it. I was proud wearing it.

But at that time, the movement between the Jewish and non-Jewish parts was still very free. So when I used to go with Isabela, I used to take it off. Simply because we are not allowed to go to the non-Jewish parts.

And at that time, I remember that the Polish factory, the chocolate factory, Wedel opened branches, and there were queues. And we used to go with Isabela to buy chocolates, and this was a big adventure.

I also remember that there were probably kitchens opened. And one day, Isabela took me to a public kitchen when there was a soup. And we were having the soup, which was wonderful. Those were the little vignettes.

But the restriction against the Jews was, as I mentioned, you know, the lack of employment, the [INAUDIBLE]. There were many people resettled from other places, coming into the Jewish section.

INT: How did they get along?

AH: At the beginning, it was confusion and fright. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] But at that time, there was-- they were also starting-- the sequence is a little vague in my mind, but the ghetto started. And people were coming from outside towns into the Warsaw ghetto.

And there were all kinds of rumors, and the negative rumors were dismissed. And the Jews in Warsaw felt that if all the Jews are being brought into Warsaw, this is with the intention of saving the Jews, just keeping them in one place. And this kind of hopeful thinking persisted until and even after the walls of the ghetto started to be built and the ghetto was closed.

INT: Do you remember when that was?

AH: I believe it was in 1941. At that time, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the teachers who could not teach outside of the ghetto organized themselves in the ghetto, and they created an educational system. Saying that after the war, those certificates of education would be recognized as bona fide. So we used to-- I used to go and attend classes in the regular schools that were taking place in different teachers' apartments.

My sister, in the meantime, joined the school of nursing. And since my father couldn't work anymore, and everybody had to register themselves in some kind of a profession, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] my father registered as a maker of wooden frames. And as such, he got a license to open and supply the Germans with wooden photograph frames. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I have to tell you that at that time, there were already [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] transport of Jews from Warsaw into unknown places. And only those that were quote, 'employed,' could stay. If you didn't have some kind of a piece of paper testifying that you are employed, you were taken. And the first transport that were taken were the people-- were older people, sick people who came from outside of Warsaw. Those were the first transports.

INT: Did any of you have any idea where they were being transported to?

AH: No. I remember it must have been in 1940, '41. At that time, I joined the [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Polish Youth-- Jewish Polish youth group, Hashomer Hatzair. And in 1940 or '41, two young men from Vilna, which is east Poland, came and we had a big, big conference in the big synagogue of Warsaw on Tlomackie street.

These two young men, one of them I believe was Yehuda Wengrower. And the other one, I don't remember. They were telling us about the atrocities that the Germans were perpetrating upon the Jews, about the mass killing of the Jews in the Vilna ghetto. And I remember that at the end of the conference when we all stood up to thank the Jewish [NON-ENGLISH], it was so strong that the walls shook.

Then I came home and I told my father about it, and my father dismissed it completely. And said to me, don't say anything. Don't create panic of these warmongers. And don't disturb your mother. So whether he knew and tried to deny it, I couldn't tell you. And whether my mother knew, I couldn't tell you. But we knew. And I think that this was the beginning of the seed of the Warsaw ghetto rebellion, this particular meeting.

I continue going to school. I remember also it must have been in 1942, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] when the allied forces entered the war, there was a blackout. And in Warsaw ghetto, it was all dark. And people were walking around on the street with phosphorous little [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] pins so that they could see each other walking. Otherwise, you couldn't see anything.

In Warsaw ghetto was at that time a terrible, terrible hunger. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] It was-- it was indescribable. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] There was no food coming in. There was no food in the ghetto. The rations that were received were not sufficient for anything. It was enough to have two pieces of bread per day, which you had supplement with potatoes if you could get it. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

There were children whose parents were transported out, orphans on the streets. And you could see them from day to day sitting on the streets, getting thinner and thinner, and then suddenly getting bigger. When they were in the last stage of the hunger, they were-- [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] I don't-- they were bloated. And they used to die on the street.

I remember one day when my sister and I were walking on the street, and she bought some detergent for washing. And the young-- and we were walking through. This was wrapped up. And the young man fell on her and sunk his teeth right into it, because what happened was he thought it was food. And what was happening is that the Jewish police was running after the youngsters that were assaulting people, and ripping their possessions off their hands, and confiscating it. So they have learned when they took something, immediately put it in their mouth so that it won't be taken away from them. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Also the transports were getting more and more often. We used to have my aunt, my father's sister living with us. And like many other people, because they didn't know what tomorrow will bring, people were walking around with knapsacks where they kept all of their most precious possessions in case they're going to be taken away. At least they will have it with them. And she was taken with her knapsack one day off the street, and we never saw her again.

At that time, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I was telling you that my father was registered in making this wooden frame. And he got an apartment which was vacated, an empty apartment to have his workshop in. And my father told my sister and me to start taking the wooden frames and put it there is to make it look occupied.

And one day, my sister and I were walking. It was just across the street from where we were, and there was a selection. Selection means-- not a selection. It was a run. What it meant was that the streets were cordoned off by the SS, the Polish police, and the Jewish police. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And the Germans were running on the streets and in the buildings telling people to get out. No papers would help you at that point. Everybody out, out, out.

My sister and my mother was in our apartment. My sister and I were in this empty apartment. And we started to run into a bunker, which was a hiding place where people used to hide. But because we didn't live in this building, the people from this building wouldn't let us in. So we had no choice. So we had to go back into this empty apartment and wait and see what's going to happen.

And we sat in this empty apartment. In the meantime, we could hear the screams, and yells, and whistles on the street. And people running, and screaming, and yelling. And we just sat in this empty apartment. And to-- to-- to keep each other, boost each other morale, we used to tell jokes to each other.

And as we were sitting there on the floor, there were bed bugs. And my sister wanted to kill a bed bug. And I told her, don't-- don't kill it. If you kill them, they will kill us. And we were sitting on the floor and scooting around to let the beg bugs walk around us. And all of a sudden, we hear on the stairs big sound of boots and a man's voice screaming and yelling, [GERMAN SPEECH]. Which means all Jews out, out, out.

And we were just sitting there. And suddenly, the door was pushed with a butt of a rifle. And what we didn't notice is that we were sitting behind the door. We didn't notice it. The apartment was empty. The-- the-- the door flew open. We were sitting behind the door. And this man put his head into apartment. Some went in the apartment and left.

So we're sitting there I don't know for how long. And when everything was finally quiet, we walked out. And we were the only ones left in that building, because they had found the bunker. They took everybody out. And we're the only two left alive from this house. We run across the street, and my mother didn't hear what was going on, and she was safe, too.

At that point, my father-- you know, this idea of the workshop with the frames went. You know, it was no longer valid. And my father registered as a wood carver. And as such, he got work in the German military cemetery, which was situated outside of the ghetto. His job was to carve the names and the ranks of the fallen German soldiers, their date of birth, and the date if death on the crosses that were then put in the cemetery to mark their graves.

And as such, he was working outside of the ghetto with a pass. And he was able to bring from outside food, which was wonderful. We had again bread. Sometimes we had tomato, and potatoes, and whatever it is that he could. And with this pass, he was allowed to bring those things. And this helped us in terms of food.

And one day, pass or no pass, my father was taken to Umschlagplatz. Umschlagplatz was the place where all the Jews were gathered before being transported out. My father's boss at that time was a German captain. And I wish I knew his name, but I don't.

And my sister had his number, so she called him and told him what happened. And he came immediately with his car to the Umschlagplatz. And he was talking to the young SS man that was guarding the place to release my father. And my-- the young policeman said, I don't recognize your authority. He said, look at my rank. He said, I don't care. I have to have a written order from the SS.

So there was nothing that the captain could do. And he told us that tomorrow morning he's going to come with the paper. And we're crying. We're telling him, you know, tomorrow will be too late, because the transports are going on a daily basis. But there was nothing we could do. And some-- by some kind of miracle, my father succeeded to get out of Umschlagplatz and came back home. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

He went to work the following day. And he asked the captain, who was-- who had the whole battalion of Wehrmacht soldiers housed in what used to be a public school, whether he could take my mother, my sister, and me out of the ghetto to be servants to the soldiers. To do anything they need to be done. And the captain agreed, but we had to go through to the Jewish labor exchange.

And when we went through the Jewish labor exchange, the labor exchange asked the captain to perhaps take more than three people. As a result, there were 30 people as Jews living in the basement of this public school, working as the-- the men as workers in the cemetery, and the women doing all kind of house duties. My sister and-- no, my sister remained in the ghetto. There was no room for her. It was only my mother and I. And I used to help my father carve the names on the crosses. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

My sister stayed in the ghetto. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] Because she stayed in the ghetto, I had a pass to go back and forth and bring her some food. I also had an opportunity to go back to Hashomer Hatzair. What I omitted to say is that when the Warsaw uprising started, the Hashomer Hatzair was very much involved in it.

INT: What about before the uprising? Was Hashomer Hatzair involved in any functions of self-help or--

AH: Yes. The Hashomer Hatzair was-- [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] was a socialistic movement [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] and very Zionistic. They were expert in youth education. And their whole focus was on Zionism, on Eretz Yisrael. We were taught songs. And no matter what was happening in the ghetto around us, we were kind of protected with the fantasy and the dream of living for Zionism and Eretz Yisrael.

Since of course, any kind of a political activities were banned, they were not only had to cover, but they indeed have created shelters for the orphans whose parents were deported. So they had public kitchens in their headquarters, and they were taking care of all the orphans that they could that were not in the [NON-ENGLISH] place. You know, there was no room there for everybody. So they-- this is what the Hashomer Hatzair did.

INT: And what was your role in it?

AH: My role in the organization was-- we were organized as a military kind of a movement. There were battalions and-- and small groups of six, seven. And then-- the seven little groups consisted into organization of a bigger group. It was like platoons and whatever the terms are.

My function at first was to teach Polish to the Jewish children who did not know how to speak Polish. How to read and write Polish. Then when the organization of the uprising started, my function was to go after curfew paste the-- the posters calling the Jews to armed rebellion on the walls of the buildings.

My father, of course, was-- he didn't know what I was doing. But when I was coming home after the curfew, it was-- it was a terrible thing. They were terribly, terribly worried.

INT: What was that like? How could you maneuver yourself without being caught?

AH: You were street wise. You know, there were patrols, but it was dark. And we knew the streets. And we're going through valley, through the backyards. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] You know, we're risking our lives, but we were young. And one was going with a bucket and the brush to paste the wall, and the other one was running with the roll of posters and they are clipping them. You know, it was-- it was fun. You know, it was-- we were too young, I think, to realize how dangerous it really was. It was a kind of a sport.

INT: What did it mean to you?

AH: Oh, I was adamant that the-- the order of the day from Hashomer Hatzair was that we are not going to be taken alive. You know, we are not going to be allowed to take on the transports. And I was all hyped up with it. I knew that this is exactly what I'm going to do. We are not going to go just like that. So-- there was just a conviction. We are just not going.

INT: What about the others in the ghetto, the religious community or the Judenrat?

AH: Like in any other place, there was a great generation gap in the ghetto. The young people didn't have the responsibility of their families. They can-- they could afford to be heroic. They didn't have to worry about anybody. The older people who had families couldn't afford to be to quite that heroic. They believed that maybe by building the bunkers and all kinds of hiding places, they can survive rather than to fight.

There were, of course, a Jewish council who were-- who were doing anything to appease the Germans by bribes and money. And give them money, and again money, and pay them again and again. Until one day, Czerniakow, who was then the president of the Jewish community, was asked again to give money. And there was no money. And he, in desperation, committed suicide rather then cooperate with them.

So you know, this was the mood in the ghetto. You know, it's-- we need to-- to think of the dynamic constellation of the ghetto not very different than the dynamic constellation of any other place when you have suddenly this little categories of the older people, rich people, poor people. And everybody protecting their own vested interests.

You had heroes and you had robbers. You have smugglers on a big scale that became very rich. You had the intelligentsia that could no longer-- use their intellect that became very poor. It was a-- a confusion of values. And you had your share of heroes and robbers, and people that were taking advantage of being taken advantage of. There was a market in the ghetto where people were selling their personal possessions in exchange for [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] food.

With the decrees that the [NON-ENGLISH] decreed against the Jews, and one of the things was that the Jews couldn't have pianos. And I remember my father selling the pianos. And a Pole came into our house, and he gave him some money and took-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and took-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] no.

He gave him some money, and my sister was supposed to bring him the three legs of the piano to get the rest of the money. And she did. She went on to the other side. There was one of the passages that you can still go from the ghetto to the Aryan side through the house of the-- courthouse that was on the border of the ghetto and the Aryan side.

And she took the three legs, and she went to collect the money. She got the money. And as she was coming back, the people that were supposed to transport the piano were going after her. They robbed her of the money. And they told her, if you don't give us some money, we will tell-- we will give you to the SS men telling them that you are Jewish. So no legs, no money, no piano. That was just of a little incident.INT: Tape three, Mrs. Heilman. What were the sanitary conditions in the ghetto?

AH: The sanitary conditions were awful. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Obviously, there was no hot water. A cake of soap was equivalent to, to a cake of gold. And the overpopulation was tremendous. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] The malnutrition, [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] all of this combined together caused an epidemic of typhoid.

When there was-- at the beginning, when there was a case of typhoid, the whole apartment building was blocked off and disinfected and people quarantined. But after a while, it became such an epidemic that all systems collapsed, and people were infecting one another.

This was-- this is a special kind of typhoid. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's not the stomach typhoid. It was the skin typhoid, very, very infectious. And people were infecting each other. Many people died, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] quote, natural death. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] It was horrible. To maintain any kind of cleanliness was very, very difficult.

INT: What sustained you?

AH: My mother. My mother. She made absolutely sure that we are dressed properly, that we are cleaned properly.

My father, I remember, made a shoe polish out of grease, ashes, and something black so that when we were walking out, we were-- our shoes were polished and we were dressed properly. And there were soldiers, I told you before. And we had to appear properly dressed. And we did. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] My parents were very exceptional people.

INT: You were talking about Hashomer Hatzair before. How did the resistance start?

AH: As I mentioned to you before, the seed of the resistance started at this big conference on the-- in the synagogue. There were several different youth organizations in the ghetto. I couldn't tell you what kind. But I myself was familiar with Hashomer Hatzair. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

I am not aware of the policy and overall strategy. I was not part of the top hierarchy of the resistance. I was just a rookie. But I do know that the plans for the resistance of different youth organizations have taken responsibility for different graphical areas of ghetto so that each one was responsible for that particular area.

INT: And what was Hashomer responsible for?

AH: The particular street, the area where their headquarter was, was from [NON-ENGLISH] to [NON-ENGLISH]. Further east, I believe, somewhere in [NON-ENGLISH], was different organization. I really couldn't tell you exactly. But I remember on one blockade-- it was blockade, by the way, the word that I was looking for before and couldn't remember where they were blocking the street and transporting the people out.

On one blockade, I was with three or four people. Only one of them had a rifle. And we were just rookies. One of them was a messenger. We didn't have telephones. We didn't have walkie-talkies. So young boys were running from one post to another to tell them what the story is.

And in this particular group, there was the commandant with our big group by the name of Shimon Heller. One of the messenger boys was running from the street and was shot. So Shimon ran after him to pick him off the street, and he was killed on the spot. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Then we had another runner who told us to abandon them at that post and run to another spot. What was happening is that the gang, we knew the buildings, the ruins of the building, and the alleys and we're running from post to post while the Germans were shooting down on the street. We could see them.

INT: What was it like to see Jews fighting Germans?

AH: I don't think that it was a question at the point of Jews fighting Germans. It was us not going without resistance. You know, whoever they were-- there were Germans. There were Ukranians. There were Poles. We were not going to, to go without resistance. And this felt very good and very frightening at the same time.

INT: And how long did this go on?

AH: This went on for quite a while. But I wasn't there at all times. I was, as you remember, with my parents outside. I used to come in only sometimes.

And at one point I was told that they cannot accept guests for security reasons. I was kicked out. I could no longer participate.

INT: Where were you?

AH: I was with my parents on the Aryan side. And I used to come in to visit my sister. And at those occasions, I used to join them until one particular moment, they told me that I can no longer come, you know? Either I-- they gave me a choice. Either I am with them or not. And this was from one of the most agonizing decisions that I had to do in my life because I couldn't abandon my parents.

INT: What about you sister?

AH: My sister was still in the ghetto. And in one of the big fights-- in the big resistance movement-- this must have-- this must have been around the Passover time. We run with one of the runners from Hashomer Hatzaie through the canals, through the burning ghetto at that time. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] What was happening at that time was that we ran into our apartment.

The Germans were methodically exploding and burning all the buildings in the ghetto, one building after another. We were in our apartment on the third floor on the three-story building. Two buildings away from us was burning. The heat and the smoke from that building was so suffocating and so hot that we couldn't stay in this apartment anymore.

The smock was intense. Downstairs in the yard there were Ukrainians and Germans with rifles. They were shooting. And they were screaming, people out, people out.

We jumped from the third floor windows into the yard, into the [INAUDIBLE]. But the smoke were so intense, and people were jumping from all over the place, that they were shooting indiscriminately. And we succeeded to run. We had scraped knees and elbows and nothing else.

And we ran through the aisle, through the ruins of the burning building with this one runner through the canals. And when we were running through the canals, we could feel the gas being pumped into the canals behind us. But we succeeded to get out on the Aryan side. And we ran to join our parents. And we stayed there until May 3, 1943.

INT: What happened then?

AH: The ghetto was finished, quote. And we were told by our captain that the Germans will come and take us. He gave us a way out. We were forewarned. My father was trying to make some arrangements to have us hidden on the Aryan side under non-Jewish papers. But before we could get organized, the SS came and transported us to the Umschlagplatz.

We were coming, walking through the still burning ruins of the ghetto to the Umschlagplatz. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] There was a horrible stench, a very peculiar kind of a stench of burning flesh, rubber. What used to be a ghetto was totally ruins. There were mountains of stone, smoke. And you could see people jumping from one pile to another and disappearing.

It was a [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] indescribable scene. When we came to Umschlagplatz, there was a sea of humanity. People were sitting on their parcels holding them. And there were-- the Umschlagplatz was also in one of those buildings that used to be, before the war, a public school. They were all built in the same kind of a system.

I don't know how long we were there. [PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS] One day? I don't know. It was overnight, two nights, two days, what. From there we were taken to the waiting train.

INT: How many people were there around?

AH: In the Umschlagplatz?

INT: How many Jews?

AH: I don't know. 1000? More? I don't know. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] We were taken out to the cattle train. And we were packed. I know that in our wagon, there were 150 people because they were counting how many. And 150, and they closed the doors.

If you think of a cattle wagon, I don't know the exact dimension. But this is about 3/4 [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] of a city bus at the most, like a city bus. And if you can imagine 150 people, each one dragging a possession that looks like a knapsack with whatever they had.

INT: Were you together with you parents and your sister?

AH: Yeah. This was my parents, and my sister, and I. At the beginning, there were some people that were trying to avoid panic and asked people to sit if at all possible so that-- there were two small little windows-- to let the air come in. But it didn't take long that people were in a panic.

And the younger-- the younger and the stronger monopolized the little windows. People were trampling on each other. And I remember my sister and I were bodily keeping people from trampling my mother. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I don't know how long this trip took. But when we came to a forking of the road, again there was some expert that were telling us that we are not going to Treblinka, which was well known by that time. It was a death camp. We're going to Auschwitz. And this was the better of the two. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

There was no food. There were no toilet facilities. And there was no water. And this was worst of all. The thirst was terrible.

Anyway, our train came to Majdanek. And as we came out, half of the people were dead and half of the people were mad. It was raining when we arrived to Majdanek. And the Germans with rifles and dogs barking were waiting for us. And again, there was screaming and yelling. Out! Out! Out!

We had to jump. It was quite high from the wagons to the ground. And I remember I leaned down to scoop up a little bit of mud to quench my thirst. And I got a whip on my back.

And the screaming was run, run, run. And we were running. We were running through an obstacle course because you couldn't run with all the belongings weighing on you. I threw out my rucksack. And everybody before and after me were throwing whatever they had. So all this possession was thrown on the ground. My mother kept her rucksack with her.

And we came to a spot where a young SS man called me. I left my mother, and I went over to him. And he pointed with a finger to go in to the right. And I went to pick my mother up. And he said, no, no. And my mother went straight. And this was the last time I saw her.

I was taken to a sauna, which is a German for a washroom. I forgot to tell you that the men and women were separated as we came from the train. So I lost my father right then and there.

And in the sauna, I have found my sister, whom I have lost on the way. So the two of us were there. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] This was the last time both of us saw our parents. This was it.

So we were in Majdanek. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] We were told to take off all our clothes, but we could take our shoes. Went through cold water showers and we were given some clothes to wear that. And we're taken into the camp. Those were wooden barracks, brand new. They smelled wonderful with fresh wood. And there was nothing else there. We slept on the floor.

And every day we used to go out on appels, which are the role calls. And we're working in the field. And I remember we were asking-- they were all Czech prisoners. We were asking them where our parents were.

And they were very kind. They used to tell us that we are young and strong. And we work hard work, but our parents have easier work somewhere in other camps. And we believed them. So we volunteered to work out in the fields by any chance maybe to see them. Well, we never did.

In Majdanek when we're working in the fields, they had horseradish growing. So we used to eat the horseradish, which was, you know, something good to eat.

We didn't stay in Majdanek very long. It was-- we arrived there in May. We left, I believe, in September. We arrived to Birkenau. I believe it was September. I'm not quite sure.

INT: How did you get to Birkenau?

AH: By train. But it was nothing like the transport from Warsaw to Majdanek. Don't even remember the details. It was nothing special.

INT: What happened at Birkenau?

AH: At Birkenau, [PAUSES FOR 9 SECONDS] the first thing that was done, our hair was shorn. Our numbers were tattooed on our left arm. We went through the showers.

And then we were given a Russian prisoner of war's uniforms to wear. Obviously, there were Russian prisoners there before us. And this is what we were given to wear.

INT: What was it like to have your number tattooed on your arm?

AH: I think that at that point we were so numb that we reacted more to our hair being shorn than to the number. We couldn't recognize each other without our hair. The numbers were tattooed by other prisoners.

Some of them knew how to write, and some didn't. And lucky for me, the one that tattooed my number knew how to write so that my number is quite legible. She was also a Jewish--

INT: Can you show your number?

AH: That's the number. You see, this is 48,150. The triangle denotes Jews. The non-Jews did not have a triangle. They had nothing.

This number's when-- by the number, you can tell which transport it is from, where it came, because it went in numerical order. So you know exactly which transports you came from.

It was until 1944. In 1944, they started numbers again. And they started was an A at the beginning. And they put it on the inside of the arm rather than on the outside.

The girl that tattooed my number looked at it and told me, you are going to come out of here alive. I said, how can you tell that? And he said, because the total of your numbers is 18. And 18 in Hebrew spells chai, which means life. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] From there, we were taken to camp B to a quarant-- quarantine, quarant--

INT: Quarantine?

AH: Quarantine. It took about two weeks, a month. I don't know exactly. It was a horrible scene. Quarantine means that you didn't work. You stayed there.

In the morning they had to-- we used to get up at about-- who knows-- 4 o'clock, 5 o'clock in the morning. It was pitch dark. And we're staying-- standing for hours on this famous roll call. It was bitter cold.

They were cleaning the block. So we couldn't go back in. So we were driven to what was called a Wiese in German, which is a meadow. It was just a square of sand.

And when we came, there were Greek girls before us. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They were walking corpses. We had never seen anything like it. They used to sit on the ground totally without any strength, without any energy. Their eyes were dead. Their-- [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] their bones were looking through their skin.

And their occupation was to look for lice in each other. And this was going on for hours. We were looking, keeping, and sitting. Alive corpses. It was horrible. There was a name for them in Auschwitz. Thy used to call them Muselmanns. I don't know why. This is-- was the beginning.

INT: In the barracks, were there prisoners who had been there longer, who told you what was going on or what to expect? How to--?

AH: Well, I don't remember that, but I remember those that were-- had official duties, who were sadistic, really, and very mean, who used to tell us, you look good now. Just wait. This is not a hotel. This is not a guest house. And you are to listen to us.

And we knew that there were selections. And we knew that there were lice. And we knew that in selection, if you have a little scratch, you will be sent to gas.

So when we came into the barracks, the barracks-- I don't know-- they were made out of bricks. And they had three stories opening, one on the floor, one in the middle, and one on top.

Eight of us were on each level. So there were 24 in one little space. There were about, I would say, eight, 16, 72 on each side, 64 on both sides. 64 times eight-- no. There was more. There was more.

Anyway, the first thing we did when we came to the barracks was throw out the mattresses because they were full of lice. And we were sleeping on the wooden boards so that not to have lice, eight of us. And at night, you just didn't know what to do with your legs. And somebody else's legs were in your face. And there were-- and that your legs were under you. It was terrible.

People were stealing the blankets. Before you came you had to steal somebody else's if you wanted to have one. And our most important possession was a red enamel bowl. Without this bowl and without a spoon, you wouldn't get your supper. You just wouldn't. So we used to try them. They had a little hole. We used to tie them up to our bodies to keep them so that they won't get stolen.

INT: And how much food did you receive?

AH: We received-- it's an interesting story. We were supposed to receive a fourth of very hard, small loaf of bread. But those that were in charge sometimes used to cut it into five.

They used to cut a slice in the middle and then put it back. So actually we got only a fifth instead of a fourth of a bread. Well, this was a reality of life.INT: Mrs. Heilman, in Birkenau, did friendships develop among the prisoners?

AH: Yes, I-- indeed they did. I think that everybody who lost a family needed to replace it. Indeed, if you did not have a friend, you did not have a zeal for life. I think that friendships and taking care of somebody other than yourself made a great deal of difference. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Perhaps I could go back to the Lager B when we were transferred [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] to Lager A, which used to be men's lager before. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We were told that we should volunteer for a-- as a metallurgist. We didn't know what metallurgists were, but we decided we're going to be metallurgists. And--

INT: Who's 'we'?

AH: My sister and I. And as metallurgists, we were-- [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] we were then scheduled to work. What we didn't know but eventually knew-- that was a munition factory.

In the transfer from Lager A to Lager B, we were in a block whose Blockalteste, or responsible for the block, was a non-Jewish woman by the name of Maria, who eventually became a kapo of the camp.

At that time, my sister became sick with typhoid. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] There was a kind of a hospital in the camp. But anybody who was in the hospital was doomed and was taken to the gas right away. So we decided not to let her go to the hospital.

And it so happened that there was a market in Birkenau. People, particularly the German prisoners, used to get parcels from home. And after working between two blocks, they used to sell the goodies from their parcels for bread or soup. Since my sister is so very sick-- was so very sick, and she couldn't eat either the bread nor the soup, I used to run to the market and get her, you know, sometimes an apple and sometimes a little bit of sugar, and whatever I could find.

And the Blockalteste was very, very kind to her. The roll calls were going on for hours and hours and hours. And she used to keep her in the block for the last-- till the last minute and let her go out only when necessary. And in this way, she helped her get over the illness.

From that block, we were transferred to another block. I believe it was block number eight, where the union workers were. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Before-- the union factory was just being built. It wasn't ready yet.

In the meantime, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] everybody that was in a Kommando, or in a special department, used to go in the morning for work. And those that weren't assigned stayed in the block. However, from time to time, they needed people to work around the camp to do whatever cleaning there was to be done. And nobody wanted to work, so we used to run away.

And I used to hide behind the door-- the front door of the block [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] under a little window where the bread was being stored. And the woman that was responsible for that was always chasing me out of there because they were responsible for the quota of workers to give them. And I hated them with a passionate hatred.

This became my very good friend, Marta, whom we got to know. She was from Slovakia. We became very, very friendly. And eventually, she saved my life.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

My sister was still weak from the sickness. And through the offices of Marta, she was given a job, which was to guard the door at night. And for that, she-- my sister received then a payment-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] a cup of jam. So we were very lucky. We had jam.

And so we ran to the market-- there was another market from the girls who were working in quote, 'Kanada.' This Kommando was called because Canada was supposed to be the richest country in the world. And these girls were working in sorting of the effects from the transport.

And they used to smuggle into the camp anything you can dream of, from silk stockings to silk underwear to, you know, whatever people use to take with them and they managed to smuggle into. Anyway, we went to the market with our cup of jam and with a spoon. And for three spoons of jam, we got our first toothbrush.

And then we couldn't understand how come the whole cup was only three spoons. And then we became wise in commerce. What you do, you choose a smaller spoon, and you dilute the jam with water. This way, you could get another toothbrush and two bras. So those were the first purchases that we made.

And we became friendly with Marta. It was friendship that lasts until today.

INT: What about news from the outside? Did you know what was going on with the war effort?

AH: Yes. There was another thing that happened. There was a group of girls that used to get together, tell stories about each other, sing Hebrew songs, dream of Israel. And somehow, we lifted ourselves from the reality of the camp into the fantasy of our meetings. And they became more real to us than the reality around us. In this--

INT: How were you able to meet? How were you able to meet?

AH: After work, you could go to each other's block. There was a period before the curfew that you could go and visit each other. And we used to meet.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

There were also news. There were girls-- there were, in this group, actually, there were Jewish girls and non-Jewish girls, as well. I remember there was a Yugoslavian girl among us. And she seemed to have the news from outside.

There was-- there was always a way to know. We knew what was going on outside. We knew, certainly, when the Germans were beginning to lose the war. And this was, of course, for us, great news.

So we knew what was going on. And in this particular group, we also learned that the war is coming to an end. This had to be in beginning of '44.

We learned that partisan-- Polish partisans from the outside and the Underground in the inside are preparing a revolt, and that the Germans are-- when the Germans are losing, they are going to rebel against it. And we knew about it.

And in this particular group, we started to think, well, what can we do in order to help? And what we did was, we secured keys from the agricultural tools [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] storeroom so that we could take rakes and hoes and whatever to, to help ourselves. And there's-- we also decided that, you know, we can-- we have access to the gunpowder, and we can start smuggling the gunpowder.

INT: So what were you-- what was your job?

AH: We were working-- at this time, we were already working in the munition factory. My sister was working in the [NON-ENGLISH], which was the gunpowder room. Their job was to fill a certain part of whatever it is that was manufactured there with the gunpowder. There were only six girls working in this gunpowder room.

I was working two department away from her in a little-- in a big room. But my job was to measure with a precision instrument a certain part of bakelite, which was called [NON-ENGLISH], for a specific indentation in it. This was my job.

INT: How was the smuggling going on? What was the route of the gunpowder?

AH: Yeah. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I was very curious. And although we were not allowed to get up from our seat and go to the bathroom-- only with permission, after a while, you got accustomed to the place. And I used to go around the factory, just to look around at what was going on.

I used to take two little metal boxes that were used for garbage and walk around as if I was, you know, doing something. And eventually, I used to take these two boxes to my sister. She used to put a little bit of gunpowder, wrapped up in a little rag tied with a string, into a box and put garbage on the top box.

And I was walking with these two boxes from my place to her door, and from her door back into my place, and put it under the table and put it inside the [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] cuff of my dress because we didn't have pockets. From there, I used to go to the washroom and in the washroom, share this gunpowder with another girl, with Ella.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

On the way from the factory-- it was about three kilometers to Birkenau where we lived-- we used to carry this on our bodies. From time to time, there were searches. When we heard that there was a search, we used to unwrap this gunpowder, throw it on the ground, and mix it with our feet on the ground so it was not distinguishable from the dirt underfoot.

INT: And if there was no search?

AH: Then we used to bring it to Birkenau. I'd give it to my sister. And my sister gave it to-- I don't know. Either directly to Roza Robota or to somebody else-- I don't know exactly who gave it to Roza Robota.

INT: And from Roza Robota, it went--

AH: It went to a special hiding place. Roza Robota had contact with the men from the crematorium. And they had approval letters to come into the women's camp. And in that particular spot, they used to come and pick it up and bring it into the crematorium.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

In October '44, there was a revolt in the crematorium. The Sonderkommandos-- those were the people who were manning the crematorium-- knew that from time to time, after a certain period of work, they were being murdered, as not to bear witnesses. And that particular group decided that they are going to rebel.

They used this gunpowder and manufactured little hand grenades made out of metal around boxes of shoe polish with a wick and filled with gunpowder. And when you lit it, it exploded. I don't know how much damage it did or it didn't.

This revolt-- it took place on October 7, 1944-- was aborted. Either they were betrayed or whatever. All the Sonderkommando people were killed. But the crematorium was destroyed as well. So there were four crematoriums in Birkenau, and one was destroyed by this.

After the revolt, the Germans found these little handmade grenades and they identified the gunpowder, which, of course, we didn't know that gunpowder had some kind of special characteristic. They identified this gunpowder as coming only from the union and the only from [GERMAN], where my sister worked.

They [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] started an investigation. They imprisoned four girls-- Roza Robota, Ella Gartner, Regina Safirsztain, and Ester Wajcblum. They tortured them mercilessly and eventually hanged them publicly on January 5, 1945.

INT: Did you witness your sister's hanging?

AH: No, I didn't. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] During the period of my sister being incarcerated in the bunker which was a prison within the prison, Marta succeeded to bring me letters from her and from Jakob, who was the kapo of this bunker. He was very, very sympathetic to the girls.

[BACKGROUND NOISE]

INT: He was sympathetic to the girls?

AH: And through him, I received letters--

