UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKER: Hello?

INT: You were saying that Marta brought letters?

AH: Marta brought, brought letters. And she was trying to get me to see my sister. But it never materialized. But Marta, sawing my depression, arranged with bribes to put me in a hospital in Auschwitz.

At this point, the union commander was in Auschwitz. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And as she was trying to reassure me and tell me-- apparently Jakob [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] received an order to execute the girls. But he was stalling for time.

And he refused. He had the power to refuse it. And he said he's not going to execute all the order and-- unless he's going to get it from Berlin. So it lasted for a while.

And finally the order from Berlin came, and he didn't have a choice. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] Marta was always coming and visiting me every day. And she was telling me that there was no order, that they are going-- that everything is going to be OK. And she wouldn't tell me the truth.

On the day of the execution, I was in the hospital. And Marta came to take me out. The union commander was released earlier to witness the execution.

But Marta's friends, [NON-ENGLISH] and [NON-ENGLISH], grabbed me and put me in the block, so that I wouldn't have to witness it. And I didn't know what was going on. I suspected, but I wasn't sure.

And when we were on the block and I wanted to run out, they bodily held me. They wouldn't let me out. And I heard this collective groan. And I knew what happened. So I wasn't-- I didn't witness it with my eyes, but I was there.

INT: What were the last words your sister left you with?

AH: Now, I don't remember it by heart. But I have it written, her last letter that I got through Marta, in which she is saying [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] that all hope is gone, that through her window of the cell, she can see the feet of the prisoners that are coming back from work, that all the hated sounds of the camp life-- the screaming for the soup and for the tea that was so hateful to her-- were so dear to her now, that all hope is gone, and that she so much wants to live.

INT: How did you get through all that?

AH: I lost my mind immediately after that. The execution was on January 5th. The evacuation of Auschwitz was on January 18, 1945. When--

INT: 1940--?

AH: Five. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] The Russians liberated Auschwitz on January 19-27, but the Germans evacuated Auschwitz on January 18, when we went on the death march. I didn't want to move from Auschwitz. I really didn't care what was going on around me.

And Marta promised Ester that she is going to take care of me. And she bodily took me from Auschwitz. And we were walking. See, it was the death march.

INT: How old were you?

AH: At that point, I was 16.

INT: And your sister? When she was executed, how old was she?

AH: Oh, she was just barely 20. Her birthday was on January 16. She was executed on January 5th. Some people, according to the calendarium of Auschwitz claim it was January 6th. Marta and I remember it on January 5th-- whatever.

INT: Can you describe the death march?

AH: I was totally out of my mind. I didn't know what was going on. And I was fascinated with the beauty of it. It was very cold. It was snowing, and it was beautiful. It was just like a Christmas picture-postcard kind of a view, all white with the little houses, with their beautiful trees. And we're just walking.

The people that were guarding us were the Wehrmacht soldiers at this point, who were unfit to serve at the front. So they were walking us. But they were old and weak, and they couldn't walk.

So we used to help them. What they wanted was walking with us. I remember we used to help him walk because he couldn't.

[PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

We used to sleep in-- with the cows in barns. And we didn't have any food. So we used to steal the cow's food, which was turnips. And we'd, we'd feed those turnips until a certain point when we were taken by a train to Ravensbruck, which was in Germany.

INT: And what happened there?

AH: By the way, during the march, we could have easily escaped. But with-- there was nowhere to go. So we-- so we continued walking.

INT: Did you meet any of the local population?

AH: No. Not that I remember of-- now. It wasn't so good for everybody. There were other people who were walking with soldiers with dogs. And any stragglers were executed on the spot. But you know, I was just out of it.

INT: What happened in Ravensbruck?

AH: At Ravensbruck? We were there, I think, only overnight. And from there, we were transported by train to Neustadt-Glewe, which, I believe, is near Hamburg somewhere. And this was a little camp where we were liberated eventually.

INT: Who were the liberators?

AH: I think that the Russians and the British met there.

INT: What did liberation mean for you?

AH: Nothing. I was very sick. I remember we were in the camp. And there was nobody around. And we're sitting there.

In Auschwitz, we were afraid to stay because we were told that Auschwitz was mined, and it will explode, that the Germans would explode the camp. So this is why we went. In Neustadt-Glewe, we didn't know anything. And there was-- I was very sick, both mentally and physically. I was rotten from the waist down with boils.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] so-- when there was nobody around, finally-- we were sitting and sitting, and finally, some brave girls thought to go out. There was nobody around. So they raided the storerooms with food. There was powdered milk and powdered eggs.

And they simply started to gorge themselves. And unfortunately, many of them died with overeating. I was too sick to go. So maybe this is why I survived.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Near our camp was a camp for SS men. And Marta and I got in there. And we got a hot shower. There was still hot water in this SS men's room. So the first thing we did was take a hot shower in this room.

Then [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we were transported into the town of Neustadt-Glewe. And very quickly, there were repatriation process. Next to me in union factory was a Belgian girl who was telling us how good the chocolate and ice cream was in Belgium.

So Marta wanted to go back to Slovakia to try and find her family. And I said to her, come on, let's go and try the ice cream first. And as a result, we went to Belgium. And both of us were in Belgium after liberation.

INT: How long did you stay in Belgium?

AH: For about a year. Marta stayed in Belgium, and I immigrated to Israel.

INT: And how were you able to rebuild your life?

AH: While I was in Belgium, we were sent to a-- by the Jewish community to a kind of a summer camp. And in this camp, I was writing a diary. And I wrote whatever I could remember up until the execution.

I think that writing this diary helped me [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] come to terms. I left the life behind, the, the camp life, and started life again. Mind you, I was very, very depressed for a long time before I started to rebuild it.

I found the loss of Marta as the ultimate loss. It was somebody from the past. I was very young. I was 16 at this point. And I took it badly.

INT: The loss of--?

AH: Marta-- in a sense, because she got married and stayed in Belgium when I--

INT: Went to Israel.

AH: --went to Israel. In Israel, in 1947, I met my husband. We married, had two children, went back to school, and lived ever after.

INT: And, and you studied?

AH: Social work.

INT: And have you worked in that profession?

AH: I worked in this profession for-- in Israel, in Boston, and in Canada for 30 years.

INT: And did you ever find out news about any other members of your family?

AH: I-- when I came to Israel, I was reunited with my sister, who left in 1939. She lives there with-- now, her husband. And we were reunited in Israel.INT: You mentioned that you got married and had children. How do you think your experiences have affected the way you brought up your children?

AH: Oh, I don't know. I don't know. Who can tell? [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] When we lived in Israel, Israel was going through a very dynamic changes of its own.

And we, of course, were caught up in the stream of life. Whatever happened before was totally forgotten. There was no time for that. 1948 was the war of independence.

There were hardships and exhilaration of the independence of the state of Israel. And nobody had really the luxury at the time to stand by or to ruminate about the past. We were just swept by the stream of life in Israel.

I went back to school. And I worked as a social worker in 1951. My first daughter was born in 1953. My second daughter was born.

And we just lived a full life, just like anybody else. In 1958, we immigrated to the states. In 1960, we came to Canada. Both in the States and in Canada I was working. I was a full time worker and mother, a wife. We were both working very hard.

And whatever was, was. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] We didn't particularly hide from the children that I am a survivor. They knew my number. They lived with this.

We used to drive them to a very [NON-ENGLISH], which they were reluctant to go to because there were just a few old people. They weren't interested, particularly.

But through the years, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they grew with it. My sister and law was in Auschwitz, too. She has a number, too. They had very close contact with her.

It was a part of reality. It was nothing that was over emphasized or under emphasized. Nevertheless, I am sure that they knew perhaps subconsciously not to ask too much.

I don't know. It's very hard to tell you. All I can tell you is that they're both very supportive. They're both very Jewish. That they went with me to Auschwitz. That they were with me when there was a monument commemorating the former events in Israel always beside me.

Both of them were quite active. Actually, one of them started the second generation in Ottawa with the first taping. So I don't know how to answer you.

INT: You mentioned a commemoration in Israel.

AH: Yes.

INT: How did that come about?

AH: In 19-- I believe it was 1982 or 1988. I'm not sure which. I corresponded with Marta. I correspond until this day.

Marta was telling me that she has read a great deal about writings about [NON-ENGLISH] and her role in the resistance in Auschwitz. And she was telling me that high time has come to mention the three other girls who were forgotten by history.

I couldn't be bothered. I was too busy. I was working. I just didn't want to.

In 1988, Marta came to Canada with the express reason to tell me and to order me, get up and do. So I did. After all, I owe her my life.

Whatever Marta said, I had to do. And since 1988, and-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we started the air force. First of all, there was a Mr. Ernst Kuhn, a survivor from Auschwitz, who has discovered that the bicycle factory in Frondenberg, in Germany belongs to the same people and is directed by the same people who directed the union factory in Auschwitz.

He decided to bring them to court and demand reparation for the workers of the union factory. He erected the plaque for the four girls in Frondenberg in the cemetery. Unfortunately, he died without accomplishing his task.

After that event, there started to be an organization called the Union Survivor's Committee in Israel, who felt that since there is a plaque in Germany, there also should be something in Yad Vashem to recognize the four girls. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They worked very, very hard for a number of years until finally succeeding in erecting a monument for the four girls in the commemorative gardens in Yad Vashem in Jerusalem.

Following that, efforts were made by the same group with the chair person by the name of Lidia Vago to have the Auschwitz Museum recognize the four girls. And through their hard work, in 1994, a plaque commemorating the four Jewish heroines has been erected on the block 27 in Auschwitz. A block which is commemorated to the Jewish martyrdom during the Second World War.

Following that, the Polish government has-- is honoring the four girls by giving them posthumously the Order of Valour, of Polish Valour.

INT: And that will be when?

AH: And this will be in March 21st, 1996 in the Polish embassy in Ottawa. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Following that, efforts are being made to correct the exhibit in the Memorial Museum in Washington, which [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] so far is exhibiting only one picture instead of all four. They do cover the event. But they do not cover it by their exhibit. And efforts are being made to have them corrected.

INT: If you could talk to the younger generation today, what message would you want to leave?

AH: I don't know. I will tell you why. I'm very confused. [PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS] There is no question in my mind that when you meet with adversity of life, you have to stand tall. And you have to do what's right, because you can't divorce yourself from yourself.

By the same token, I paid too big a price. Perhaps if we weren't so right, my sister would be still alive. What was the price and for what? Did the world change? The world is not going to change unless we believe in changing the world, but we have to pay a very, very high price.

I don't know what to tell you. I don't know. I know that for me, the answer is to stand for what I believe in. I don't know if this is done for everybody. I don't know. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I wouldn't tell this to the young people, but I'm telling this to you. I don't know.

INT: Have you been involved in educational programs?

AH: Yes. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Since Marta came to talk to me, I didn't stop talking. I went with [NON-ENGLISH] in 1993 to Auschwitz. I spoke to the university students. I spoke in the March of the Living. I spoke in the video program that was made by the Chicago Holocaust community about resistance. I [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] am speaking occasionally in the schools in Ottawa to the young people.

INT: What does it mean to you?

AH: It tears me apart. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But I do it because I want to believe that through the process of education, these young people will prevent the evil that was done, and can be done, and is being done. That by knowing to what extremes a totalitarian regime can bring, that they will stand up and be able to say no. And believe in themselves, and say there are certain things they are ready to do. And there are certain things that they will not allow to be done. I hope so.

INT: What was it like for you at the commemoration at Yad Vashem?

AH: It was a very spiritual experience. Marta was there. My sister, Sabina, was there. We were honored during the ceremony to light the eternal flame, both my sister and I.

And as I bent down to light the flame, I saw both my parents smiling at me. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] When we went back into the line, my sister bent over to me and said to me, do you know that our parents were here? So we both experienced the presence of our parents there.

After the ceremony, I was talking to my younger daughter. And they told her, look, this is what happened. But they-- wait. I don't understand. Why didn't-- why wasn't Estusha there? And she said to me, why don't you understand? Her spirit is still alive.

INT: Thank you.

AH: You're welcome.

